CORE libstdcxx
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ copy throws: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ noexcept\(int\): SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ throwing \(long\): SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The built-in __is_nothrow_constructible(T, Args...) with arguments was
true for every T.  It now constructs T from the arguments, as
__is_constructible does, and checks the exception specification of the
selected constructor.
